  [1mThe consequence:[0m session lifetime is the window an attacker has with a stolen session token — a lifted cookie, a
  shared or unlocked machine, a session hijacked via XSS. Going 30m → 8h widens that window 16x, and it also means an
  idle session on an unattended device stays live for a full workday instead of expiring over a coffee break.
  [38;5;153mserver.js:2[39m treats this as the session expiry directly, so there's no secondary revocation path in this code to soften
  it.

  To be clear, I'm not saying don't do it — 8h is a common, deliberate choice for internal tools where re-login friction
  costs more than the risk. I just need you to make that call, not me.
